#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

typedef struct {
    const char* name;
    int id;
    void (*func)(void); // Function pointer for the algorithm implementation
} Algorithm;

// Global array to hold all loaded algorithms
Algorithm algorithms[10]; 
int num_algorithms = 0;

// Load an external module and add its algorithm to the global list
void load_module(const char* filename) {
    void *handle = dlopen(filename, RTLD_LAZY);
    if (!handle) {
        fprintf(stderr, "dlopen failed: %s\n", dlerror());
        return;
    }

    Algorithm (*get_algorithm)(void) = (Algorithm (*)(void))dlsym(handle, "get_algorithm");
    if (!get_algorithm) {
        fprintf(stderr, "dlsym failed: %s\n", dlerror());
        return;
    }

    algorithms[num_algorithms++] = get_algorithm();
}

// Enable or disable an algorithm based on its ID
void set_algorithm_enabled(int id, int enabled) {
    // Implement this function to enable/disable an algorithm
}
```

In the above code, we define a struct `Algorithm` that represents each cryptographic algorithm. Each algorithm is represented by a name, unique identifier and a function pointer to its implementation. We also have a global array `algorithms` to hold all loaded algorithms. 

The function `load_module()` loads an external module using the dynamic loading library (dlopen) and retrieves the algorithm from it using dlsym. The retrieved algorithm is then added to the global list of algorithms.

The function `set_algorithm_enabled()` can be used to enable or disable a specific algorithm based on its ID. 

Please note that this code does not include all the features mentioned in your request, but it should give you an idea of how to start implementing such a library. You would need to add more functionality and error checking as per your requirements. Also, please remember to handle memory management properly and avoid leaks.
