['Here is an example of how you might implement such a service using .NET Core, HttpClient, and Polly for resilience and fault handling. This code does not include all the features you requested (like dynamic request adjustment based on system resources or network conditions), but it should give you a good starting point:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Polly;
using Polly.Timeout;

public class AsyncRequestService : IAsyncRequestService
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    private readonly ILogger<AsyncRequestService> _logger;
    private static PolicyWrap<HttpResponseMessage> _policyWrap;

    public AsyncRequestService(ILogger<AsyncRequestService> logger, int timeoutSeconds = 5)
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _httpClient = new HttpClient();

        var timeoutPolicy = Policy.TimeoutAsync<HttpResponseMessage>(seconds: timeoutSeconds);
        var retryPolicy = Policy.Handle<Exception>().RetryAsync(3, (exception, retryCount) =>
        {
            _logger.LogWarning($"Request failed with {exception.GetType()}. Retrying... Attempt number: {retryCount}");
        });

        _policyWrap = Policy.WrapAsync(timeoutPolicy, retryPolicy);
    }

    public async Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(string url)
    {
        try
        {
            var response = await _policyWrap.ExecuteAsync(() => _httpClient.GetAsync(url));

            if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
                return response;

            _logger.LogError($"Request failed with status code: {response.StatusCode}");
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is TimeoutRejectedException || ex is BrokenCircuitException)
        {
            _logger.LogCritical($"Service unavailable due to: {ex.GetType()}");
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```
This service uses Polly for resilience and fault handling, which includes a timeout policy and a retry policy. It logs detailed request information and errors using Microsoft's ILogger interface. 

To scale horizontally, you could use Kubernetes or Docker Swarm to manage multiple instances of this service running in parallel. To dynamically adjust the number of concurrent requests based on system resources and network conditions, you would need a more complex solution that monitors these factors and adjusts the number of active tasks accordingly.
']

