["This is a complex task that requires multiple components including the RESTful API, JSON schema validation, dependency tracking, concurrency control, caching strategies, error handling, and performance optimization. Here's an example of how you might structure your code using Node.js with Express for the API, Ajv for JSON schema validation, MongoDB as a database, and Redis for caching:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
const Ajv = require('ajv');
const ajv = new Ajv({allErrors: true});
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const redis = require('redis');
const client = redis.createClient();

// Define your JSON schema and compile it
const schema = {...}; // Your JSON schema here
const validate = ajv.compile(schema);

// Connect to MongoDB
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost/test', {useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true});

// Define your Mongoose model
const Resource = mongoose.model('Resource', new Schema({...})); // Your schema here

app.put('/resource/:uuid', async (req, res) => {
  const valid = validate(req.body);
  if (!valid) {
    return res.status(400).json(validate.errors);
  }

  // Check for active references before updating
  const referencingEntities = await Resource.find({references: req.params.uuid});
  if (referencingEntities.length > 0) {
    return res.status(409).json({message: 'Active references exist', entities: referencingEntities});
  }

  // Use Mongoose's findOneAndUpdate to handle concurrency
  const updatedResource = await Resource.findOneAndUpdate(
    {uuid: req.params.uuid},
    {$inc: {version: 1}, $set: req.body},
    {new: true, runValidators: true}
  );

  // Invalidate cache for this resource
  client.del(req.params.uuid);

  res.json(updatedResource);
});

app.delete('/resource/:uuid', async (req, res) => {
  const resource = await Resource.findOne({uuid: req.params.uuid});

  // Check for active references before deletion
  if (resource.references.length > 0) {
    return res.status(409).json({message: 'Active dependencies exist', entities: resource.references});
  }

  await Resource.deleteOne({uuid: req.params.uuid});

  // Return a new resource with default values
  const newResource = new Resource();
  res.status(204).json(newResource);
});
```

This is just a basic example and doesn't include all the details you requested, such as handling large datasets or unexpected spikes in traffic. Also, it assumes that you have a MongoDB database with a Resource model defined, which includes fields for UUID, version number, references, etc. You would need to replace `{...}` with your actual schema and JSON schema definition.
"]

['Here is an example of how you might implement this in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;
using System.Xml.XPath;

public class ConfigManager
{
    private static readonly object _lock = new object();

    public string GetSetting(string directoryName, string configFilePath, string settingKey)
    {
        try
        {
            if (!File.Exists(configFilePath))
                throw new Exception("Configuration file does not exist.");

            var dirInfo = Directory.CreateDirectory(directoryName);
            if (!dirInfo.Exists)
                throw new Exception("Failed to create directory.");

            lock (_lock)
            {
                Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable("CONFIG_DIR", dirInfo.FullName, EnvironmentVariableTarget.Process);
            }

            var doc = new XPathDocument(configFilePath);
            var navigator = doc.CreateNavigator();
            var iterator = navigator.Select($"//*[local-name()='{settingKey}']");

            if (!iterator.MoveNext())
                throw new Exception("Setting not found.");

            string settingValue;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                settingValue = iterator.Current.Value;
            }

            return settingValue;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error: {ex.Message}");
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a `ConfigManager` class with a single method, `GetSetting`, which performs the operations described in your task. It uses locks to ensure thread-safe access to shared resources and handles exceptions gracefully by logging them and returning default values where necessary. The XPath parsing is used to retrieve the setting value from the configuration file.
