["Here is a basic implementation of such function in JavaScript using a caching mechanism and event listeners:

```javascript
class DOMTraverser {
    constructor() {
        this.cache = {};
        this.listeners = [];

        // Register event listeners for dynamic content updates
        document.addEventListener('DOMNodeInserted', () => this.updateCache());
        document.addEventListener('DOMNodeRemoved', () => this.updateCache());
        document.addEventListener('DOMSubtreeModified', () => this.updateCache());
    }

    // Function to traverse the DOM tree and return matching elements
    query(selectors, options = {}) {
        const cacheKey = selectors.join();

        if (this.cache[cacheKey]) {
            return this.filterElements(this.cache[cacheKey], options);
        } else {
            let elements = [];

            // Traverse the DOM tree and collect matching elements
            for (let selector of selectors) {
                elements = [...elements, ...Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selector))];
            }

            this.cache[cacheKey] = elements;

            return this.filterElements(this.cache[cacheKey], options);
        }
    }

    // Function to filter and limit the number of returned elements
    filterElements(elements, options) {
        let filteredElements = [...elements];

        if (options.maxResults && options.maxResults < filteredElements.length) {
            filteredElements = filteredElements.slice(0, options.maxResults);
        }

        return filteredElements;
    }

    // Function to update the cache when there are changes in the DOM tree
    updateCache() {
        this.cache = {};
    }
}
```

This code creates a `DOMTraverser` class that maintains a cache of selectors and their corresponding elements. When a query is made, it first checks if the result is already cached. If not, it traverses the DOM tree to find matching elements and caches them for future use. It also registers event listeners for dynamic content updates (like new nodes being inserted or removed) to update the cache accordingly.

Please note that this code does not handle third-party scripts modifying elements unexpectedly. For such cases, you might need to isolate your logic or use event-driven mechanisms. Also, please consider the performance implications of different traversal orders (depth-first vs. breadth-first) and choose the most appropriate approach based on your specific requirements.
