['Here's an example of how you might implement this function using Java's built-in `Files` class to traverse directories and `Path` class for handling paths. This code also uses a `HashSet` to store unique file/directory paths, which ensures that we only count each path once. 

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.io.*;

public class FileProcessor {
    private static final Set<String> visitedPaths = new HashSet<>();

    public static int processFile(String root, String configFile) throws IOException {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        Path rootPath = Paths.get(root);
        if (!Files.exists(rootPath)) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException("Root directory does not exist: " + root);
        }

        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(Paths.get(configFile))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                Path path = rootPath.resolve(line).normalize();

                if (!Files.exists(path)) {
                    System.err.println("Warning: File/directory does not exist: " + line);
                    continue;
                }

                String absolutePathStr = path.toAbsolutePath().toString();
                if (visitedPaths.contains(absolutePathStr)) {
                    continue;
                }

                visitedPaths.add(absolutePathStr);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new IOException("Error reading configuration file: " + configFile, e);
        }

        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("Traversal time: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");

        return visitedPaths.size();
    }
}
```
This function reads the configuration file line by line, resolves each path relative to the root directory and normalizes it. It then checks if the resolved path exists and whether we've already visited this path before. If not, it adds the absolute path string to our set of visited paths. 

The time taken for traversal is logged at the end. The function throws exceptions when encountering non-critical failures (e.g., if the root directory or configuration file do not exist). It also logs warnings for non-existent files/directories in the configuration file, but continues processing other entries.
']

['Here is a basic implementation of such a service using Java NIO. This code does not include all the features you requested like dynamic scaling, integration with an external configuration management system or detailed error handling. It's just a starting point for your requirements. 

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class NIOServer {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(NIOServer.class.getName());
    private Selector selector;
    private ServerSocketChannel serverSocket;
    private ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(2048);

    public NIOServer() throws IOException {
        this.selector = Selector.open();
        this.serverSocket = ServerSocketChannel.open();
        this.serverSocket.bind(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 5454));
        this.serverSocket.configureBlocking(false);
        this.serverSocket.register(this.selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);
    }

    public void start() throws IOException {
        while (true) {
            this.selector.select();
            Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = this.selector.selectedKeys();
            Iterator<SelectionKey> iter = selectedKeys.iterator();
            while (iter.hasNext()) {
                SelectionKey key = iter.next();
                if (key.isAcceptable()) {
                    register(this.serverSocket.accept());
                }
                if (key.isReadable()) {
                    read((SocketChannel) key.channel());
                }
                iter.remove();
            }
        }
    }

    private void register(SocketChannel socketChannel) throws IOException {
        socketChannel.configureBlocking(false);
        socketChannel.register(this.selector, SelectionKey.OP_READ);
    }

    private void read(SocketChannel socketChannel) throws IOException {
        this.buffer.clear();
        int read = socketChannel.read(this.buffer);
        if (read == -1) {
            socketChannel.close();
            socketChannel.keyFor(this.selector).cancel();
            logger.info("Socket closed");
            return;
        }
        this.buffer.flip();
        while (this.buffer.hasRemaining()) {
            System.out.print((char) this.buffer.get());
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a non-blocking server that listens for incoming connections and reads data from them. It uses the Java NIO Selector to handle multiple concurrent connections efficiently. 

Please note that this is a very basic implementation and does not include all of your requirements. For example, it doesn't support dynamic scaling or integration with an external configuration management system. You would need to add these features yourself based on your specific needs. Also, you should handle exceptions properly in production code. 

Also note that this is a blocking I/O server. If you want to use non-blocking I/O, you will have to use CompletionHandler and AsynchronousSocketChannel instead of SocketChannel. This would require more complex code and understanding of Java NIO.
']

