```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct State {
    int value;
    struct State* next;
} State;

typedef struct FSA {
    // Add fields for the FSA structure here
} FSA;

// Assuming you have a function to check if a state is valid based on the transition rules of an FSA
int isValidState(FSA* fsa, int current_state, int next_state) {
    // Implement your own logic here
    return 1;
}

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Recursive function to traverse the FSA and create a new state list
State* createStateList(int* states, int numStates, FSA* fsa, int lastState, int maxDepth) {
    if (numStates <= 0 || maxDepth < 0) return NULL;

    // Lock mutex to ensure thread safety
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    State* newState = malloc(sizeof(State));
    if (!newState) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return NULL;
    }

    // Assign value to the new state and set next pointer to NULL for now
    newState->value = states[0];
    newState->next = NULL;

    if (isValidState(fsa, lastState, newState->value)) {
        State* remainingStates = createStateList(&states[1], numStates - 1, fsa, newState->value, maxDepth - 1);

        // If the recursive call returned NULL, free the allocated memory and return NULL
        if (!remainingStates) {
            free(newState);
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return NULL;
        }

        newState->next = remainingStates;
    } else {
        // If the state is not valid, free the allocated memory and return NULL
        free(newState);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return NULL;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return newState;
}
```
This code uses a mutex to ensure thread safety. It also checks if the state is valid before recursively calling `createStateList`, and frees any allocated memory if an invalid state is encountered. This approach minimizes memory usage during traversal by only allocating memory for valid states. 

Please note that this code does not handle circular references within the FSA or update the 'next' pointer of the last state in the list to point to the newly created state list, as these requirements were not specified in the problem statement. You would need to add these features based on your specific needs and constraints. 

Also, this code does not handle stack overflow or excessive memory consumption issues. In a production environment, you may want to use an iterative approach instead of recursion for better control over memory usage and potential stack overflows. However, the exact implementation would depend on your specific requirements and constraints.
