['Here is an example of how you might approach this problem in Java. This code uses Google's Gson library for deserialization, Apache Commons IOUtils for efficient byte array handling, and Spring Retry for retry mechanism with exponential backoff. 

```java
import com.google.gson.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.IOUtils;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import java.lang.reflect.Type;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class DeserializationService {
    private final Gson gson = new Gson();

    @Retryable(value = {RuntimeException.class}, maxAttempts = 5, backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000))
    public <T> T deserializeAndInstantiate(byte[] data, Class<T> interfaceClass) throws RuntimeException {
        try {
            String jsonString = IOUtils.toString(data, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

            Type typeOfSrc = new TypeToken<JsonObject>(){}.getType();
            JsonObject jsonObj = gson.fromJson(jsonString, typeOfSrc);

            String className = jsonObj.get("className").getAsString();
            Class<?> clazz = Class.forName(className);

            if (!interfaceClass.isAssignableFrom(clazz)) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Deserialized class does not implement the specified interface");
            }

            return gson.fromJson(jsonString, clazz);
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to load class", e);
        }
    }
}
```
This code assumes that your serialized data includes a "className" field which contains the fully qualified name of the class to be deserialized. It also uses Gson's TypeToken to correctly handle generic types when deserializing. 

