['Here's a basic implementation of your requirements using C language. This code does not include all the details you mentioned like integration with external services or handling network conditions but it gives an idea about how you can implement this in C.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    int priority;
} Transaction;

typedef struct {
    Transaction transactions[5000]; // assuming 10,000 transactions per second is possible
    size_t count;
    clock_t startTime;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} TransactionBuffer;

TransactionBuffer buffer = { .count = 0, .startTime = clock(), .lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER };

void processTransactions() {
    while (1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&buffer.lock);

        if (buffer.count > 0 && ((double)(clock() - buffer.startTime)) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC >= 5) {
            // process transactions in the buffer
            for (size_t i = 0; i < buffer.count; ++i) {
                printf("Processing transaction with id: %d and priority: %d\n", buffer.transactions[i].id, buffer.transactions[i].priority);
            }

            // reset the buffer
            buffer.count = 0;
            buffer.startTime = clock();
        }

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&buffer.lock);
    }
}

void addTransaction(int id, int priority) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&buffer.lock);

    if (buffer.count < sizeof(buffer.transactions) / sizeof(Transaction)) {
        buffer.transactions[buffer.count].id = id;
        buffer.transactions[buffer.count].priority = priority;
        ++buffer.count;
    } else {
        printf("Buffer is full, dropping transaction with id: %d\n", id);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&buffer.lock);
}

int main() {
    // start the processing thread
    pthread_t thread;
    pthread_create(&thread, NULL, (void *(*)(void *))processTransactions, NULL);

    // add some transactions
    for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
        addTransaction(i, rand() % 10);
    }

    pthread_join(thread, NULL);

    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a buffer to hold transactions for the last 5 seconds and processes them when the time window is up. It uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing the shared buffer. The `addTransaction` function adds a transaction to the buffer, dropping it if the buffer is full. The `processTransactions` function processes all transactions in the buffer and resets the buffer.

Please note that this code does not include integration with external services for configuration updates or handling network conditions. You would need to add these features based on your specific requirements. Also, you may need to adjust the size of the transaction buffer depending on your expected transaction volume and rate limits.
']

