['Here is a basic implementation of such a function in C using pthread library for thread safety and locking mechanisms. This code does not include all the features you mentioned like dependency management, performance constraints, scalability, usability, maintainability, integration issues etc. These are complex topics that require careful design and testing to ensure they work correctly in your specific use case.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    unsigned short RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    assert(newNode != NULL);

    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void remove(unsigned short RNTI) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node** indirect = &head;

    while(*indirect != NULL) {
        if((*indirect)->data.RNTI == RNTI) {
            Node* nextNode = (*indirect)->next;
            free(*indirect);
            *indirect = nextNode;
            break;
        }

        indirect = &(*indirect)->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void clear() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    while(head != NULL) {
        Node* nextNode = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = nextNode;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code provides a basic thread-safe linked list implementation. It uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the list at a time, preventing race conditions and ensuring data consistency. The `add` function adds a new element to the head of the list, while the `remove` function removes an element with a specific RNTI value from the list. The `clear` function clears the entire list without losing the ability to append new elements.
']

