['Here's an example of how you might implement this in C# using HttpClient and async/await patterns. This code does not include all the features mentioned in your requirements (like dynamic retry interval adjustment based on server responsiveness, detailed logging, periodic reports generation, performance bottleneck detection, integration with external monitoring tools), but it provides a good starting point for handling asynchronous HTTP requests under dynamic network conditions and varying server responsiveness.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class AsyncRequestProcessor
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    private readonly int _maxConcurrencyLevel;
    private readonly TimeSpan _connectionTimeout;
    private readonly int _errorThreshold;
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _concurrencySemaphore;

    public AsyncRequestProcessor(int maxConcurrencyLevel, TimeSpan connectionTimeout, int errorThreshold)
    {
        _httpClient = new HttpClient();
        _maxConcurrencyLevel = maxConcurrencyLevel;
        _connectionTimeout = connectionTimeout;
        _errorThreshold = errorThreshold;
        _concurrencySemaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(maxConcurrencyLevel, maxConcurrencyLevel);
    }

    public async Task<List<HttpResponseMessage>> ProcessRequestsAsync(IEnumerable<string> urls)
    {
        var tasks = new List<Task<HttpResponseMessage>>();
        foreach (var url in urls)
        {
            await _concurrencySemaphore.WaitAsync();
            tasks.Add(ProcessUrlAsync(url));
        }

        var results = await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
        return new List<HttpResponseMessage>(results);
    }

    private async Task<HttpResponseMessage> ProcessUrlAsync(string url)
    {
        try
        {
            _httpClient.Timeout = _connectionTimeout;
            var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync(url);
            return response;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error processing URL: {url}. Exception: {ex}");
            throw;
        }
        finally
        {
            _concurrencySemaphore.Release();
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a class `AsyncRequestProcessor` that accepts the maximum concurrency level, connection timeout, and error threshold as parameters in its constructor. It uses a SemaphoreSlim to limit the number of concurrent requests according to the specified maxConcurrencyLevel. The `ProcessRequestsAsync` method processes each URL asynchronously using the `ProcessUrlAsync` method, which includes error handling and resource cleanup.
']

