['Here is a basic Rust code snippet that implements a network packet processing function using Union-Find data structure for managing unique packets. However, please note that this is a simplified version and does not cover all the requirements mentioned above. 

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
use std::process::id;

struct UnionFind {
    parent: HashMap<u32, u32>,
}

impl UnionFind {
    fn new() -> Self {
        UnionFind { parent: HashMap::new() }
    }

    fn find(&mut self, i: u32) -> u32 {
        let j = *self.parent.entry(i).or_insert(i);
        if i != j {
            let k = self.find(j);
            self.parent.insert(i, k);
            k
        } else {
            i
        }
    }

    fn union(&mut self, i: u32, j: u32) {
        let i = self.find(i);
        let j = self.find(j);
        self.parent.insert(i, j);
    }
}

struct PacketProcessor {
    union_find: UnionFind,
    packet_times: Vec<Instant>,
    packet_sizes: Vec<usize>,
    start_time: Instant,
    prev_size: usize,
}

impl PacketProcessor {
    fn new() -> Self {
        PacketProcessor {
            union_find: UnionFind::new(),
            packet_times: Vec::new(),
            packet_sizes: Vec::new(),
            start_time: Instant::now(),
            prev_size: 0,
        }
    }

    fn process(&mut self, packet: Packet) {
        let now = Instant::now();
        if !self.union_find.parent.contains_key(&packet.id) {
            self.union_find.parent.insert(packet.id, packet.id);
            self.packet_times.push(now);
            self.packet_sizes.push(packet.size);
        }
    }

    fn get_metrics(&mut self) -> (Duration, usize, usize) {
        let elapsed = self.start_time.elapsed();
        let size = id().expect("Failed to get process ID").as_raw_fd() as usize;
        let packet_rate = self.packet_times.len() as f64 / elapsed.as_secs() as f64;
        (elapsed, size, packet_rate)
    }
}
```

This code does not cover all the requirements mentioned above such as system dependencies, performance constraints, scalability and usability. It's a starting point for further development based on your specific needs. 

Please note that Rust is a low-level language and it doesn't have built-in support for network packet processing or Union-Find data structure. You may need to use external libraries or write these functionalities yourself, which can be complex tasks depending on the complexity of your requirements.
