['Here is a basic implementation of the task in C# using ASP.NET Core MVC. This code assumes you have an `IPageService` interface with methods for updating HTML content, checking permissions, etc. 

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using System;

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class PageController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IPageService _pageService;
    private readonly ILogger<PageController> _logger;

    public PageController(IPageService pageService, ILogger<PageController> logger)
    {
        _pageService = pageService;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPost("{sectionId}/{elementId}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> UpdateElement(string sectionId, string elementId, [FromBody] string htmlContent)
    {
        try
        {
            // Check user permissions
            if (!_pageService.HasEditPermissions(sectionId))
                return Unauthorized();

            // Get the latest version of HTML content from the service
            var latestVersion = await _pageService.GetLatestHtmlContentAsync(elementId);

            // Compare versions to prevent data loss
            if (!_pageService.IsLatestVersion(latestVersion, htmlContent))
                return Conflict("The page has been updated since you last loaded it. Please refresh and try again.");

            // Update the HTML content using a service class
            await _pageService.UpdateHtmlContentAsync(elementId, htmlContent);

            // Log the change
            _logger.LogInformation($"User {User.Identity.Name} updated element {elementId} in section {sectionId}.");

            return Ok("The page should be refreshed to reflect the changes.");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Log any errors and return a 500 Internal Server Error response
            _logger.LogError(ex, $"An error occurred while updating element {elementId} in section {sectionId}.");
            return StatusCode(500, "An unexpected error occurred. Please try again later.");
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses ASP.NET Core's built-in dependency injection to inject the `IPageService` and `ILogger<PageController>` dependencies. It also handles exceptions by logging them and returning a 500 Internal Server Error response, which is appropriate for unexpected errors.

For concurrency management, you might consider using optimistic concurrency control (OCC) or pessimistic locking depending on your specific use case. For example, you could add a `version` column to the database table and increment it every time an update occurs. This way, if two users try to edit the same section simultaneously, only the one with the latest version will succeed.

For performance optimization, consider caching strategies such as in-memory caching or distributed caching depending on your specific use case. For example, you could cache the latest HTML content for each element and invalidate it when an update occurs. This way, you can avoid hitting the database every time a user loads a page section.

For error handling and resilience, consider using Polly or similar libraries to implement retry policies and circuit breakers. These can help your application recover from transient errors and prevent cascading failures.

Finally, for log management, you might want to use a logging framework like Serilog or NLog that supports structured logging (i.e., key-value pairs) and sinks (i.e., output targets). This will make it easier to analyze logs and troubleshoot issues.
