['Here is a C code snippet that implements your requirements:

```c
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    char* string;
} record;

void* allocate_and_initialize(long offset_count, int key_count, int record_count, 
                               int string_length, int min_value, int max_value) {
    if (offset_count < 0 || key_count < 0 || record_count < 0 || 
        string_length < 0 || min_value > max_value) {
        return NULL; // Invalid input parameters
    }

    long* offsets = malloc(sizeof(long) * offset_count);
    if (offsets == NULL) return NULL; // Memory allocation failed

    int* keys = malloc(sizeof(int) * key_count);
    if (keys == NULL) {
        free(offsets);
        return NULL; // Memory allocation failed
    }

    record* records = malloc(sizeof(record) * record_count);
    if (records == NULL) {
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        return NULL; // Memory allocation failed
    }

    srand(time(NULL));
    for (int i = 0; i < record_count; ++i) {
        records[i].string = malloc(sizeof(char) * (string_length + 1));
        if (records[i].string == NULL) {
            // Memory allocation failed, clean up and return NULL
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) free(records[j].string);
            free(offsets);
            free(keys);
            free(records);
            return NULL;
        }

        // Initialize string with "PREFIX" followed by random alphanumeric characters
        strcpy(records[i].string, "PREFIX");
        for (int j = 6; j < string_length; ++j) {
            int charType = rand() % 3;
            if (charType == 0) records[i].string[j] = 'A' + rand() % 26; // Uppercase letter
            else if (charType == 1) records[i].string[j] = 'a' + rand() % 26; // Lowercase letter
            else records[i].string[j] = '0' + rand() % 10; // Digit
        }
        records[i].string[string_length] = '\0'; // Null-terminate the string

        // Set integer value of record to a random value within specified range
        records[i].value = min_value + rand() % (max_value - min_value + 1);
    }

    return records;
}

EVP_CIPHER* choose_cipher(const char* cipherName) {
    OpenSSL_add_all_algorithms();
    const EVP_CIPHER *cipher = EVP_get_cipherbyname(cipherName);

    if (!cipher) {
        printf("Error: could not find the requested cipher.\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    return (EVP_CIPHER*)cipher;
}
```
This code snippet includes a function `allocate_and_initialize` that allocates and initializes the required structures. It also includes a function `choose_cipher` to choose an encryption algorithm from OpenSSL's EVP library. The chosen cipher is returned as an `EVP_CIPHER*` pointer.
']

['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements in C# using `System.Xml` and `System.IO` namespaces. This code does not handle all the scenarios you mentioned but it gives an idea about how to approach this problem. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Xml;
using System.IO;

public class XMLParser
{
    public async Task ParseXMLFiles(string directoryPath, string xpath)
    {
        if (!Directory.Exists(directoryPath))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Invalid directory path.");
            return;
        }

        var files = Directory.GetFiles(directoryPath);

        // Use Parallel.ForEach to process multiple XML files simultaneously
        await Task.Run(() => Parallel.ForEach(files, async file =>
        {
            try
            {
                XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument();
                doc.Load(file);

                // Use SelectNodes with XPath to find matching elements
                var nodes = doc.SelectNodes(xpath);
                if (nodes == null || nodes.Count == 0)
                    return;

                foreach (XmlNode node in nodes)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("File: " + file);
                    Console.WriteLine("Element text content: " + node.InnerText);
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                // Handle XML parsing errors gracefully
                Console.WriteLine($"Error while parsing {file}: {ex.Message}");
            }
        }));
    }
}
```
This code reads all the files in a directory, loads each file into an `XmlDocument` object and uses XPath to find matching elements. It handles potential XML parsing errors gracefully by catching exceptions and printing error messages without crashing. 

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not cover all your requirements such as handling nested structures up to a depth of 5 levels, validating well-formedness before parsing, memory allocation failures, etc. You would need to add these features according to your specific needs. Also, you might want to consider using more efficient data structures or algorithms for dealing with large files and concurrent file processing under varying resource conditions.
']

