['Here is a basic implementation of your task in C++ using std::thread for concurrency, std::chrono for time management, and std::random for random selection. This code does not include external dependencies for attack animations and sound effects as it would require specific libraries or APIs to handle these tasks which are beyond the scope of this platform.

```cpp
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <mutex>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

std::mutex mtx; // Mutex for thread-safe concurrency

// Predefined attack indices
const std::vector<int> ATTACK_SEQUENCE = {0, 1, 2};

void performAttack(int playerId) {
    static int currentIndex = 0;

    // Lock the mutex to ensure thread-safe concurrency
    mtx.lock();

    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 2);

    // Select a random attack from the sequence
    int attackIndex = ATTACK_SEQUENCE[dis(gen)];

    // Unlock the mutex after performing the attack
    mtx.unlock();

    std::cout << "Player " << playerId << " performs attack " << attackIndex << "\n";
}

void manageAttackSequence() {
    while (true) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) { // Assume 10 players
            std::thread(performAttack, i).detach();
        }

        // Sleep for the cooldown period before repeating the sequence
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(9));
    }
}

int main() {
    manageAttackSequence();

    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a thread for each player to perform an attack. The mutex ensures that only one player can perform an attack at a time, preventing resource contention and ensuring smooth gameplay. The sleep_for function is used to implement the cooldown period of 9 seconds before repeating the sequence. This implementation assumes a fixed number of players (10 in this case) for simplicity but it could be easily adjusted to handle varying loads by dynamically adjusting the number of threads or using thread pools.
']

