["Here is a basic implementation of `processDocumentMetadata` function with some considerations for scalability, concurrency, performance constraints, integration, maintainability, and memory management. 

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <random>

struct Record {
    int intValue;
};

struct DocumentMetadata {
    int id;
    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, double>> attributes;
};

void processDocumentMetadata(std::vector<DocumentMetadata>& metadata, int targetId,
                             const std::vector<std::string>& attributesToUpdate,
                             bool updateExisting) {
    static std::mutex mtx; // for thread safety
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // locks the mutex

    std::unordered_map<int, DocumentMetadata*> metadataMap;
    for (auto& doc : metadata) {
        metadataMap[doc.id] = &doc;
    }

    if (metadataMap.find(targetId) != metadataMap.end()) { // target document found
        auto& targetDoc = *metadataMap[targetId];
        for (const auto& attr : attributesToUpdate) {
            bool attributeFound = false;
            for (auto& existingAttr : targetDoc.attributes) {
                if (existingAttr.first == attr) {
                    // update the value of the attribute
                    existingAttr.second = getAttributeValueFromExternalSource(attr);
                    attributeFound = true;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (!attributeFound && updateExisting) {
                targetDoc.attributes.emplace_back(attr, getAttributeValueFromExternalSource(attr));
            }
        }
    } else { // new document
        DocumentMetadata newDocument;
        newDocument.id = targetId;

        for (const auto& attr : attributesToUpdate) {
            newDocument.attributes.emplace_back(attr, getAttributeValueFromExternalSource(attr));
        }

        metadata.push_back(std::move(newDocument)); // add the new document to the vector
    }
}
```

This function uses a `unordered_map` for quick lookups of documents by their IDs, which is more efficient than searching through a vector every time. It also locks a mutex before accessing the shared data structure to ensure thread safety. 

The function assumes that there's an external source (not shown here) from where it can retrieve attribute values for updating or adding new attributes. This could be a database, a network service, etc., and is represented by `getAttributeValueFromExternalSource` which should be implemented accordingly.

Memory management is handled automatically by the C++ standard library: when a vector grows, its capacity doubles, and when it shrinks, it doesn't decrease until it reaches half of its current size. This ensures that reallocations are minimized. 

The function also uses move semantics to efficiently add new documents to the metadata vector, which can be more efficient than copying large objects.
